## SECTION 3 — AOA STAGES TABLE
<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276; width: 34%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276; width: 33%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher greets students with "Good morning, everyone! Are you ready to play?" and plays the "If You're Happy and You Know It" song, but adapts it to "If you like games and you know it, clap your hands!" to introduce the theme. The teacher displays large flashcards of school activities (art, music, math, playground) on the board and points to each one, saying its name clearly. Students repeat chorally.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up a flashcard, for example, "art." The teacher points to themselves and says, "I like art!" with a big smile and a thumbs-up gesture. Then, the teacher holds up another flashcard, for example, "math," and says, "I don't like math!" with a frown and a thumbs-down gesture. The teacher models the pronunciation of /l/ in 'like' and /g/ in 'games' by exaggerating the sounds and having students repeat 'like' and 'games' after them. The teacher also helps students recognize the sounds of letters for 'm' in 'math' and 'g' in 'games' by isolating the initial sounds.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher uses TPR and simple questions.
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Showing a thumbs-up and smiling) "This is 'like'? Yes or no?" (Expected response: "Yes!")</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Showing a thumbs-down and frowning) "This is 'like'? Or 'don't like'?" (Expected response: "'Don't like'!")</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "I Like School!".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Illustrated storybook or a sequence of large, colorful pictures depicting children engaging in different school activities and expressing preferences.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> The students will understand and identify simple expressions of likes and dislikes related to school activities.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> The teacher shows the picture story, pointing to each child and their activity. For example, "Look! This girl is painting. She says, 'I like art!'" The teacher then shows a picture of a boy at the playground. "This boy is playing. He says, 'I like playground!'" The teacher asks simple gist questions like:
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Pointing to the girl painting) "What does she like?" (Expected response: "Art!")</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Pointing to the boy playing) "Does he like the playground? Yes or no?" (Expected response: "Yes!")</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher emphasizes the pronunciation of `/l/` in "like" and `/g/` in "games" by repeating these words clearly in context.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Speaking.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Speaking about likes and dislikes using 'I like...' and 'I don't like...' with school activities vocabulary. Answering 'Do you like it?' with 'Yes' or 'No'. Grammar: Subject + verb (I like school), Present simple (I like games), Yes/No questions. Vocabulary: art, music, math, playground, science, English.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice - "Like or Don't Like?" Game (Choral Repetition & TPR):</b> The teacher holds up flashcards of school activities (art, music, math, playground, English, science). For each flashcard, the teacher asks, "Do you like [activity]?" Students respond chorally using TPR: thumbs up and "I like [activity]!" or thumbs down and "I don't like [activity]!" The teacher ensures students articulate /l/ in 'like' and the initial sound of 'math' and 'games' accurately.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice - "Teacher Asks, You Answer" (Individual Responses):</b> The teacher randomly picks flashcards and asks individual students, "Do you like [activity]?" The chosen student responds with "Yes!" (and a nod) or "No!" (and a head shake). The teacher encourages pointing to the picture as they speak. For example, "Yes! I like music!" (pointing to the music flashcard).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction - "Ask Your Friend" (Guided Peer Check):</b> Students work in pairs. Each pair receives a set of 3-4 small flashcards depicting different school activities. Student A picks a card and asks Student B, "Do you like [activity]?" Student B responds with "Yes, I like [activity]!" or "No, I don't like [activity]!" accompanied by a thumbs-up or thumbs-down. They then switch roles. The teacher circulates, monitoring pronunciation and correct use of target phrases, and providing assistance as needed.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "I Like School!".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> "My Favorite Game/Activity" Circle Share.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b> Students sit in a circle. The teacher places all school activity flashcards (art, music, math, playground, science, English) in the center. The teacher models first: "I like music! It's so fun!" (pointing to the music card). Then, the teacher encourages each child to choose one flashcard that represents their favorite school activity or game. Each student holds their chosen flashcard and, when it's their turn, stands up, shows their card to the class, and says, "I like [activity]!" or "My favorite is [activity]!" while pointing to their card. They can also add a simple adjective if they remember, e.g., "I like art! It's colorful!" The teacher facilitates, prompting students with "What do you like?" and offering praise. This activity encourages sharing preferences (sociolinguistic competence) in a low-stress, interactive environment.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the "My Favorite Game/Activity" Circle Share.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students will participate in the circle share, verbally identifying their preferred school activity using the target phrases.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation of target vocabulary (e.g., 'like', 'games', activity names).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of target vocabulary (e.g., 'art', 'music', 'playground').</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity in expressing a preference (e.g., 'I like [activity]').</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to respond to 'Do you like it?' with 'yes' or 'no' and gestures.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Collaboration and active participation in peer interactions.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> The teacher asks students to show a thumbs-up if they can say what they like, and a thumbs-down if they need more practice. Then the teacher asks, "Can you tell a friend, 'I like art'?" and "Can you say 'yes' or 'no'?"</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What was your favorite game or activity today?</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Was it easy or difficult to tell your friends what you like?</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> Most students actively participated in the "My Favorite Game/Activity" circle share, successfully using "I like [activity]!" and responding to questions. The TPR for 'like' and 'don't like' was very effective for Kinder. Pronunciation of `/l/` in 'like' and `/g/` in 'games' was generally good, but some students still need reinforcement for specific consonant sounds. The semi-controlled practice helped build confidence for individual responses. For the next lesson, more complex sentences could be introduced for expressing reasons for likes, focusing on adjectives like 'funny' or 'exciting' to expand their productive vocabulary.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> The next lesson (Lesson 4: Writing Composition) will build on the vocabulary and structures from today by having students trace or draw and label their favorite school activity, and then copy the sentence "I like [activity]!" This will solidify the understanding of expressing preferences through a different skill focus.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>